---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro'
import Diagnostic from '../components/Diagnostic.astro'
import PullQuote from '../components/PullQuote.astro'
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro'
---
<ChapterLayout
  num="01"
  slug="system"
  title="The System"
  sub="The theological intensity of modern work."
  description="Workism as secular religion, the tyranny of merit, and how self-exploitation replaced domination."
  readingTime="~6 min read"
  sources={['thompson_workism', 'han_burnout', 'sandel_merit', 'graeber_bs']}
>
  <article class="prose-faust max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <p>
      We replaced the worship of gods with the worship of potential. Derek Thompson calls it
      <strong>workism</strong><Cite id="thompson_workism" /> — the belief that work is not merely
      necessary to economic production, but the centerpiece of one’s identity and the primary vehicle
      for meaning, community, and transcendence. For a certain class of educated professional, the
      office is the last cathedral standing.
    </p>
    <p>
      This did not happen by accident. As the older containers of meaning — religion, civic
      institutions, extended family, stable geography — thinned out, something had to absorb the
      existential load. Work volunteered. It offers a scoreboard, a congregation, a moral vocabulary
      (“impact,” “mission,” “hustle”), and the promise that if you are good enough, you will be saved.
    </p>

    <PullQuote cite="Byung-Chul Han, The Burnout Society">
      The achievement-subject exploits itself. Exploitation is now possible even without domination.
    </PullQuote>

    <p>
      Byung-Chul Han’s insight<Cite id="han_burnout" /> is the hinge of this entire project. The
      factory needed an overseer. The achievement society needs none. We have internalized the
      manager. We volunteer for the overtime, optimize our own sleep to produce more, and experience
      burnout not as something done <em>to</em> us but as a personal failure of will. The prison
      guard has been dissolved into the prisoner.
    </p>

    <h2>The Tyranny of Merit</h2>
    <p>
      Michael Sandel argues<Cite id="sandel_merit" /> that the rhetoric of “rising” — that talent
      plus effort earns you your place — produces a hubris among winners and a humiliation among
      everyone else. If success is purely your own doing, then failure must be your own fault. The
      meritocratic promise quietly rewrites bad luck and structural disadvantage as moral deficiency.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why a missed promotion doesn’t feel like a bad quarter; it feels like a verdict.
      Meritocracy doesn’t just distribute jobs. It distributes <strong>self-worth</strong>, and it
      does so on terms the market sets.
    </p>

    <h2>The Substitution</h2>
    <p>
      David Graeber went further<Cite id="graeber_bs" />: a startling share of jobs are, by the
      admission of the people doing them, pointless — and yet we cling to them as identity anyway.
      The devotion is not always to the <em>work</em>. It is to what the work protects us from: the
      unstructured question of what a life is for when no one is measuring.
    </p>

    <Diagnostic label="Diagnostic — Do you recognize the symptoms?">
      <ul>
        <li>Do you introduce yourself by your job title before your name has settled?</li>
        <li>Does unstructured time produce guilt rather than rest?</li>
        <li>Is your primary community the people you work with?</li>
        <li>Would a layoff feel less like a financial event and more like an identity collapse?</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 font-mono text-sm text-cream/70">
        The more you answered yes, the more work has become your church. That is not a personal
        flaw. It is the system working as designed.
      </p>
    </Diagnostic>

    <p>
      None of this makes ambition irrational. In the cities where this story unfolds, extreme effort
      is often the mathematically correct response to a hostile structure. That is the trap we turn
      to next: a strategy that is economically sound and psychologically ruinous at the same time.
    </p>
  </article>
</ChapterLayout>
